# Content is a source. Rendering is disposable.
A presentation is a text file agents write, and one HTML file renders.

---
intent: reveal
eyebrow: What you are looking at
notes: Three numbers, the middle one is the point: nothing to install.
---
## One file, no server, no install.

::: metrics
- 1 | file | Parser, theme, fonts, linter and print, in a single HTML.
- 0 | dependencies | Opens in any browser, offline. {.hero}
- 4 | blocks in J0 | metrics, timeline, compare, statement. Ten in V1.
:::

---
intent: explain
eyebrow: How a deck gets made
notes: The agent never sees pixels. It reads the linter and fixes the source.
---
## The agent writes, the linter answers.

::: timeline
- Write | An agent, or you, writes the .jmd. Markdown plus a few blocks.
- Lint | Unknown block, too many items, real overflow measured in the DOM. Errors block the export.
- Render | The theme lays it out on-brand. No position, no size, no color in the source. {.now}
- Ship | Print to PDF, or send the self-contained .james-jmd.html. The source travels with it.
:::

---
intent: decide
eyebrow: Why not the usual tools
notes: Be fair to Google Slides. The point is who can read and diff the file.
---
## Slides editor, or source file.

::: compare
### A slides editor
- Every layout is yours to fix, one box at a time.
- The file is a binary or a blob of XML. Agents choke on it.
- The brand drifts one deck at a time.

### A .jmd source
- The theme owns the layout. You own the words.
- Plain text: diffable, versioned, written by any LLM.
- One brand file, every deck on-brand.
:::

